1
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,657
Hi everyone, welcome to the age when anyone can make a game.

2
00:00:12,677 --> 00:00:18,887
This Gintama story game first grew out of a video.

3
00:00:18,907 --> 00:00:22,157
On the 25th I asked Claude to make a pixel animation,

4
00:00:22,177 --> 00:00:23,753
called "Gintama in 300 Seconds."

5
00:00:23,773 --> 00:00:26,917
I turned on Ultra Code mode and used the Opus 5.5 model.

6
00:00:26,937 --> 00:00:30,207
I told it: make a pixel-art Gintama of the classic scenes,

7
00:00:30,227 --> 00:00:33,233
list them in order, then tell the story.

8
00:00:33,253 --> 00:00:36,307
It split the story into 15 scenes and drew 42 characters,

9
00:00:36,327 --> 00:00:38,683
plus a whole series of side views, animations and more.

10
00:00:38,703 --> 00:00:41,223
The music and sound effects were all written by it, too.

11
00:00:41,243 --> 00:00:42,813
When I later looked at its assets, I started wondering,

12
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:44,547
could this become a game?

13
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:46,137
Some friends also said they wanted to play it.

14
00:00:46,157 --> 00:00:48,513
So I reused its assets and made this game.

15
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:50,983
It has 15 chapters of story, over 600 lines of dialogue,

16
00:00:51,003 --> 00:00:53,273
and over 10,000 lines of code, all written by AI.

17
00:00:53,293 --> 00:00:57,090
From the very start to the last chapter took me under 20 hours.

18
00:00:57,110 --> 00:00:59,263
In a moment you can see it in action.

19
00:00:59,283 --> 00:01:03,223
And at the end of the video, I want to share something more important than this game.

20
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:06,013
OK, let's look at the actual gameplay.

21
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:09,713
It has some fun little touches,

22
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:17,663
like cutting the shogun's hair, dodging roadblocks on a scooter, playing pachinko.

23
00:01:17,683 --> 00:01:19,663
I didn't add many skills,

24
00:01:19,683 --> 00:01:24,373
just a basic attack and a jump for the character.

25
00:01:24,393 --> 00:01:28,860
Most of it is story, environmental dialogue, and NPC dialogue.

26
00:01:29,013 --> 00:01:33,107
It's just a web page's code file: double-click and you can play.

27
00:01:33,127 --> 00:01:35,867
Later I want to see if I can put it on Xiaohongshu,

28
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:39,243
or as a mini-program or tool on Douyin,

29
00:01:39,263 --> 00:01:40,507
so everyone can play it.

30
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:44,313
It's a side-scrolling story brawler with a three-hit combo and a jump,

31
00:01:44,333 --> 00:01:45,497
and you hold L to block.

32
00:01:45,517 --> 00:01:49,023
The scenes are interactive: you can wander around and chat with NPCs,

33
00:01:49,043 --> 00:01:51,597
with small stories and little touches,

34
00:01:51,617 --> 00:01:52,207
and a health bar.

35
00:01:52,227 --> 00:01:55,753
If your health runs out, you start over.

36
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:59,513
So let's look at where these assets came from.

37
00:01:59,533 --> 00:02:02,147
The art was all drawn by the AI itself.

38
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:06,527
It drew 42 characters, each with 31 actions and 11 expressions,

39
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:08,730
13,124 frames in total.

40
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:10,207
The backgrounds were drawn in code, too,

41
00:02:10,227 --> 00:02:11,977
and I never gave it any reference images.

42
00:02:11,997 --> 00:02:15,930
There are also 12 pieces of 8-bit music and 80-plus sound effects,

43
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:17,450
all made by it.

44
00:02:17,693 --> 00:02:19,413
Next, the story design.

45
00:02:19,433 --> 00:02:25,277
For the story design, I wanted to start from the scenes of the original anime,

46
00:02:25,297 --> 00:02:29,473
There are 15 big scenes, and each scene is one chapter.

47
00:02:29,493 --> 00:02:32,113
Every chapter has a shot list:

48
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:34,473
which second to push in, which character stands where,

49
00:02:34,493 --> 00:02:37,950
when the sound-effect text pops up,

50
00:02:37,970 --> 00:02:40,150
all recorded in dedicated files.

51
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:42,573
And the famous scenes had to be in there too,

52
00:02:42,593 --> 00:02:44,733
like the eye close-ups,

53
00:02:44,753 --> 00:02:45,663
punching through a wall,

54
00:02:45,683 --> 00:02:47,647
and "All of Edo, assemble!"

55
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:52,150
The mini-games and puzzles are a tribute to those wonderfully absurd anime scenes.

56
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:54,310
And then there's the dialogue.

57
00:02:54,330 --> 00:02:56,770
Claude wrote it to fit each character's personality,

58
00:02:56,790 --> 00:02:58,907
like "Natural Perm" and "Lake Toya,"

59
00:02:58,927 --> 00:03:00,483
"It's not a wig, it's Katsura!"

60
00:03:00,503 --> 00:03:02,707
"Zura janai, Katsura da!"

61
00:03:02,727 --> 00:03:05,707
And some catchphrases, too. I think they're pretty fun.

62
00:03:06,027 --> 00:03:09,733
Take chapter one's puzzle, plus chapter two, the family restaurant:

63
00:03:09,753 --> 00:03:13,310
The street has 8 NPCs and 10 places you can investigate.

64
00:03:13,330 --> 00:03:15,713
If you can't pay the rent, you have to dig around the office for money.

65
00:03:15,733 --> 00:03:17,593
In the sofa cushions you'll find a note:

66
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:20,740
"The money that matters is hidden behind what you value most."

67
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:23,393
Then the player has to go find what they value most,

68
00:03:23,413 --> 00:03:24,633
which is those two characters, "Sugar."

69
00:03:24,653 --> 00:03:27,357
Behind it: 5,000 yen, enough to pay the rent.

70
00:03:27,377 --> 00:03:28,337
But it turns out that's still not enough,

71
00:03:28,357 --> 00:03:30,147
and you get knocked out the door.

72
00:03:41,433 --> 00:03:47,197
For the climax, I designed a fight that runs nearly five minutes.

73
00:03:47,217 --> 00:03:50,537
There are three brawls on the rooftop, and every faction piles in at once.

74
00:03:50,557 --> 00:03:53,770
The player's Gintoki fights back to back with Takasugi,

75
00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:55,443
holding out until dawn,

76
00:03:55,463 --> 00:03:56,823
and then it goes into the finale.

77
00:03:56,843 --> 00:04:00,423
In the finale, Sadaharu bites Gintoki's head,

78
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:04,570
a nod to the beginning, when Sadaharu first came to the house,

79
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:07,063
and in the end Gintoki has to kick that "END" away.

80
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:08,590
Gintama never ends.

81
00:04:08,610 --> 00:04:10,327
It's a lot of fun.

82
00:04:10,347 --> 00:04:13,377
Making something like this isn't actually that hard,

83
00:04:13,397 --> 00:04:15,257
because I've also made many other anime videos,

84
00:04:15,277 --> 00:04:17,530
like Attack on Titan, Naruto, and Mushoku Tensei,

85
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:19,473
and all those assets can be reused

86
00:04:19,493 --> 00:04:20,463
to make games.

87
00:04:20,483 --> 00:04:24,003
So the question is: what's left of the barrier to making games?

88
00:04:24,023 --> 00:04:26,940
Before, you had to draw, write code, and compose music,

89
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:28,257
and maybe have a whole team.

90
00:04:28,277 --> 00:04:30,457
Now all you need is what you want,

91
00:04:30,477 --> 00:04:34,233
explain it clearly, and then it gets made.

92
00:04:34,253 --> 00:04:36,449
Of course, a person still has

93
00:04:36,469 --> 00:04:38,803
to judge what's good and what's not, and choose.

94
00:04:38,823 --> 00:04:40,563
And it may not be just games:

95
00:04:40,583 --> 00:04:44,503
an article, a video, a course, a comic, a novel,

96
00:04:44,523 --> 00:04:49,673
any of them can become a medium I can accept and enjoy.

97
00:04:49,693 --> 00:04:53,113
I no longer need to slog through a huge book

98
00:04:53,133 --> 00:04:56,180
and burn a lot of time on it, which is boring, too.

99
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:00,063
I can just use AI tools to turn it into a video, or a game,

100
00:05:00,083 --> 00:05:03,710
and understand its content and meaning much faster.

101
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:06,280
Before, people adapted to the shape of knowledge;

102
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:09,330
now, knowledge can grow into the shape you like.

103
00:05:09,350 --> 00:05:12,180
Welcome to the age when anyone can make a game.
